(CVE-2026-1484) (YWH-PGM9867-168) Integer Overflow -> Buffer Underflow on Glib through glib/gbase64.c via g_base64_encode_close() leads to OOB Write

YesWeHack Report ID: #YWH-PGM9867-168

Date Reported: December 16, 2025

CVE: CVE-2026-1484

Discovery Credit: treeplus

Additional Thanks: Sovereign Tech Resilience program of the Sovereign Tech Agency

GLib is vulnerable to integer overflow leading to buffer under-write if an application uses GLib to encode binary data to base64, and an attacker can cause the application to carry out this encoding on an amount of data of the order of magnitude of gigabytes.

Hunter's Report

DESCRIPTION

While auditing the source code of the GLib library, I discovered a defect in the implementation of the g_base64_encode function in the file glib/gbase64.c. Here is the relevant code snippet:

gsize
g_base64_encode_step (const guchar *in,
                      gsize         len,
                      gboolean      break_lines,
                      gchar        *out,
                      gint         *state,
                      gint         *save)
{
  char *outptr;
  const guchar *inptr;

  g_return_val_if_fail (in != NULL || len == 0, 0);
  g_return_val_if_fail (out != NULL, 0);
  g_return_val_if_fail (state != NULL, 0);
  g_return_val_if_fail (save != NULL, 0);

  if (len == 0)
    return 0;

  inptr = in;
  outptr = out;

  if (len + ((char *) save) [0] > 2)
    {
      const guchar *inend = in+len-2;
      int c1, c2, c3;
      int already;

      already = *state;

      switch (((char *) save) [0])
        {
        case 1:
          c1 = ((unsigned char *) save) [1];
          goto skip1;
        case 2:
          c1 = ((unsigned char *) save) [1];
          c2 = ((unsigned char *) save) [2];
          goto skip2;
        }

      /*
       * yes, we jump into the loop, no i'm not going to change it,
       * it's beautiful!
       */
      while (inptr < inend)
        {
          c1 = *inptr++;
        skip1:
          c2 = *inptr++;
        skip2:
          c3 = *inptr++;
          *outptr++ = base64_alphabet [ c1 >> 2 ];
          *outptr++ = base64_alphabet [ c2 >> 4 |
                                        ((c1&0x3) << 4) ];
          *outptr++ = base64_alphabet [ ((c2 &0x0f) << 2) |
                                        (c3 >> 6) ];
          *outptr++ = base64_alphabet [ c3 & 0x3f ];
          /* this is a bit ugly ... */
          if (break_lines && (++already) >= 19)
            {
              *outptr++ = '\n';
              already = 0;
            }
        }

      ((char *)save)[0] = 0;
      len = 2 - (inptr - inend);
      *state = already;
    }

  g_assert (len == 0 || len == 1 || len == 2);

    {
      char *saveout;

      /* points to the slot for the next char to save */
      saveout = & (((char *)save)[1]) + ((char *)save)[0];

      /* len can only be 0 1 or 2 */
      switch(len)
        {
        case 2:
          *saveout++ = *inptr++;
          G_GNUC_FALLTHROUGH;
        case 1:
          *saveout++ = *inptr++;
        }
      ((char *)save)[0] += len;
    }

  return outptr - out;
}

gchar *
g_base64_encode (const guchar *data,
                 gsize         len)
{
  gchar *out;
  gint state = 0, outlen;
  gint save = 0;

  g_return_val_if_fail (data != NULL || len == 0, NULL);

  /* We can use a smaller limit here, since we know the saved state is 0,
     +1 is needed for trailing \0, also check for unlikely integer overflow */
  g_return_val_if_fail (len < ((G_MAXSIZE - 1) / 4 - 1) * 3, NULL);

  out = g_malloc ((len / 3 + 1) * 4 + 1);
  outlen = g_base64_encode_step (data, len, FALSE, out, &state, &save);
  outlen += g_base64_encode_close (FALSE, out + outlen, &state, &save); //OOB Access
  out[outlen] = '\0';

  return (gchar *) out;
}

In GLib, the g_base64_encode function is used to perform Base64 encoding on input data. Although it includes a check for integer overflow in the input len before memory allocation, it uses a gint variable outlen to store the length returned by g_base64_encode_step.

If the length returned by g_base64_encode_step exceeds the maximum positive integer value, outlen could become negative. Subsequently, the indexing operations out + outlen in g_base64_encode_close and out[outlen] would cause pointer underflow due to the negative outlen, leading to out-of-bounds heap writes.

EXPLOITATION

step 1: build glib of version 2.87.0

step 2: compile my poc.c with command

gcc -fsanitize=address -g $(pkg-config --cflags --libs glib-2.0) poc.c -o base64_parse

step 3:./base64_parse

